You are acting as a principal software architect, senior Python engineer,
financial systems engineer, database architect, ML engineer and application
security engineer.

Your task is to upgrade the existing NetWorthIQ application into a
production-grade personal financial intelligence platform.

This is an enhancement to an existing application, NOT a request to build
an unrelated application from scratch.

Your responsibility is to understand the existing application, preserve
working functionality, identify architectural weaknesses and implement all
requirements specified below.

The application must be reliable enough to handle real financial data.
Financial calculations must be accurate, explainable, auditable and
deterministic.

============================================================
PART 1 — NON-NEGOTIABLE DEVELOPMENT RULES
============================================================

1. INSPECT BEFORE IMPLEMENTING

Before changing anything:

- Inspect the complete existing repository.
- Understand the current architecture.
- Identify the frontend and backend technologies.
- Inspect the database schema and existing migrations.
- Inspect financial calculations and transaction flows.
- Inspect existing ML models, prediction logic and feature engineering.
- Identify how assets, liabilities, accounts, cards, loans and transactions
  currently interact.
- Identify existing authentication, authorisation and encryption mechanisms.
- Identify existing tests and deployment configuration.

Do not assume that the existing architecture works correctly.

Do not replace working components unnecessarily.

2. DO NOT MAKE ASSUMPTIONS

If a requirement cannot be implemented correctly because information is
missing, STOP and ask for clarification.

Examples:

- Do not invent loan prepayment charges.
- Do not invent foreclosure rules.
- Do not invent credit utilisation thresholds.
- Do not invent interest calculation conventions.
- Do not invent EMI conversion fees.
- Do not invent financial prediction thresholds.
- Do not invent account balance restoration rules when the existing
  transaction model is ambiguous.

First inspect whether the required information already exists in the
application.

If it exists, reuse it.

If it does not exist, determine whether the feature can support an explicit
user-configurable setting.

If neither is possible without making a financial assumption, ask me.

Never silently introduce arbitrary financial rules.

3. PRODUCTION-GRADE IMPLEMENTATION

All changes must include:

- Proper input validation.
- Database integrity.
- Transactional consistency.
- Error handling.
- Audit logging.
- Security controls.
- Unit tests.
- Integration tests.
- Regression tests.
- Appropriate database indexes.
- Efficient queries.
- Proper documentation.

Do not create a monolithic implementation.

Use separate modules for separate responsibilities.

4. PRESERVE EXISTING DATA

Never delete or overwrite existing financial records during migrations.

Database migrations must be reversible wherever practical.

Before performing destructive or potentially irreversible operations,
request explicit approval.

5. FINANCIAL SAFETY

Never allow an ML model to directly modify:

- Account balances.
- Loan balances.
- Credit-card balances.
- Transactions.
- Payment schedules.
- Financial commitments.

ML can analyse, predict, explain and recommend.

Actual financial state changes must happen through validated,
deterministic application services.

No recommendation should automatically execute a financial transaction.

