PART 6 — ML PREDICTION AND TRANSACTION TAG INTELLIGENCE
============================================================

Upgrade the existing ML prediction chips and spending prediction logic.

The current system must not treat a single transaction amount as a
reliable indicator of a recurring financial pattern.

A. TAG-FIRST PREDICTION

Use transaction tags as the primary features for identifying recurring
behaviour.

Examples:

- Food.
- Transportation.
- Healthcare.
- Shopping.
- Utilities.
- Education.
- Entertainment.

The model should learn patterns associated with tags rather than
blindly memorising individual transaction amounts.

B. AMOUNT-SPECIFIC PREDICTIONS

Amount-specific prediction is allowed only when sufficient evidence
exists.

Use the following eligibility conditions:

1. The same tag and amount pattern occurs in more than the configured
   percentage of the user's relevant transactions.

OR

2. The same tag and amount pattern occurs consistently at a recurring
   frequency, such as daily.

The percentage threshold and recurrence criteria must be configurable
or derived from existing documented model configuration.

Do not invent an arbitrary percentage.

If the existing system has no such configuration, ask me to approve
the proposed threshold before implementation.

C. EXAMPLE

Suppose the user has 100 transactions.

The tag "Transportation" appears 25 times.

Within those transactions, ₹100 appears only once.

The system must not conclude that the user regularly spends ₹100
on transportation.

Now suppose ₹100 appears 20 times under the Transportation tag.

This is a meaningful repeated amount pattern and may qualify for
amount-specific prediction, subject to the configured threshold.

Another example:

The user makes a ₹150 transportation transaction every day.

Even if the amount does not represent a large percentage of all
transactions, its consistent daily recurrence can qualify it as a
recurring pattern.

D. PREDICTION CHIP REQUIREMENTS

Each prediction chip must:

- Have an understandable label.
- Identify the relevant transaction tag.
- Show why the prediction exists.
- Distinguish observed behaviour from predicted behaviour.
- Show confidence only when statistically justified.
- Avoid false precision.
- Avoid making unsupported financial claims.

Example:

"Transportation spending may recur today."

Explanation:

"Transportation transactions have appeared consistently over the
previous configured observation period."

Do not display an exact amount unless the amount prediction qualifies
under the evidence rules.

E. COLD-START HANDLING

When there is insufficient historical data:

- Do not manufacture predictions.
- Display insufficient historical data.
- Use descriptive statistics where appropriate.
- Avoid displaying artificially high confidence.

